

The RIIS Manual of Style (hereafter RMOS) is a resource developed by the Ronin Institute 
of Independent Scholars (RIIS 2.0) to support authors engaged in academic writing.  
RMOS seeks in particular to be useful for independent scholars who are not required to 
follow guidelines explicitly declared by publishing houses and academic 
institutions.  This gives authors more leeway to choose their own policies, but 
also means that authors may need a checklist of guidelines to consider 
when preparing their own manuscripts.

The RMOS intends to be informative rather than proscriptive or 
normative.  It will not `i.dictate` how authors should style, organize, and
present their writing.  However, RMOS will describe specific document 
locations where stylistic conventions are relevant.  Then, RMOS 
will indicate the kinds of conventions that are `i.often` adopted 
by authors, publishers, or scholarly communities.  This will hopefully 
allow writers to make their own decisions in an informed manner.

RMOS is paired with tools that may help authors check that their 
manuscripts are consistent with their preferred style.  This includes 
the option of creating personalized guidelines, inspired by the 
`q.My Style Sheet` feature of the Chicago Manual of Style (CMOS).  
Many of the entries in RMOS will list a series of options or questions 
(instead of a single fixed rule).  Each of these corresponds to a 
choice authors might instantiate via personalized guidelines.


`quasi-section.Benefits of Stylistic and Presentation Conventions` 

Upon completing a manuscript, double-checking that the document conforms 
to personal or institutional guidelines requires extra work.  It is 
therefore appropriate to point out why stylistic conventions are worthwhile.

`enums.

`item.Credibility and Professionalism`  Readers may consciously or subconsciously 
notice inconsistencies, which could at some level affect their impression 
of the publications' credibility.  It is unfortunate if trivial matters 
of textual presentation affect readers' assessment of the actual research content.

`item.Catching Errors`  Many of the manuscript locations that are covered 
by style guidelines are also disproportionately likely to be a source of 
mistakes and corrections.  This is partly due to how document sources are 
assembled.  For instance, quotations are more likely to be copy-pasted 
from notes or from other publications than re-typed by hand.  As a result, 
stylistic or text-encoding discrepancies can be introduced into a new 
manuscript that are less obvious than typos and misspellings.

`item.Document Organization`  In the course of preparing a manuscript 
with consideration to style guidelines, authors may be 
influenced by the guidelines themselves to organize the writing process 
in a structured manner.  In general, many guidelines address the 
proper appearing and formatting of specific segments of text, such as 
chapter/section/subsection titles, citations, references, quotes, footnotes, 
and so forth.  Authors who systematically adopt style guidelines 
are also potentially more likely to leverage these specific document 
elements as a structural framework for organizing their manuscripts.

`item.Coordinating Amongst Authors (and other contributors)`  For co-authored 
publications, it is important for the writers to coordinate their 
style choices to avoid inconsistencies.  This extends to manuscripts 
where multiple individuals might take on different document-prepation roles, 
such as editor, proofreader, indexer, typesetter, or compositor.  
A definitive statement of the authors' preferred style choices can help 
those playing secondary/editorial roles to sustain the authors' preferences.

 
  
`quasi-section.A Note on Imput Sources` 

In general, RMOS discusses the kind of presentation that authors might 
aspire to.  It is outside the scope of this document to instruct 
authors on how to achieve their desired effect; for example, on how 
to set parameters such as margins or font size within compositing 
or Word Processing software.

With that said, however, RMOS will provide information targeted 
at authors who intend to build professional-quality books and articles 
rather than handing off manuscripts to commercial publishers.  In a 
typical workflow, the sources through which authors submit papers 
will be different from those used internally by the publishing house.  
An article submitted as Microsoft Word, for example, may be 
converted to JATS XML before the camera-ready final copy is produced, 
and indeed before technical editing and style-checking is carried out.  

Insofar as authors choose alternative publishing options and eschew 
both reader paywalls and Article Processing Charges (APCs), authors in 
general have to take on more of the responsibilities otherwise 
delegated to commercial publishers.  RMOS therefore recommends 
that authors use formats similar to internal publishing workflows, 
such as JATS XML, either as the initial document format or as 
part of a document-prep pipeline.  Consequently, the following entries will 
when relevant include information about JATS and related encoding 
for the segments of a manuscript covered by specific style guidelines.

Note furthermore that individual publishers often extend/customize JATS 
(and analogous formats, such as LaTeX) according to their own in-house 
preferences.  In the context of independent scholarship, authors 
may similarly take the initiative to develop a personal `q.style` 
influencing how their writings are finalized.  In this context, 
`q.style` does not refer exclusively to a `q.Manual of Style` but 
also subjective/aesthetic choice about fonts, layouts, colors, 
and other visual details, as well as computational decisions 
about how publications' contents are represented for human 
readers.  For instance, although commercial publishers usually 
curate a human-visible, back-of-book index only for full-sized 
volumes (not individual journal articles), independent authors 
may find it useful to include an index and/or glossary at the 
end of shorter publications as well.  Independent researchers 
have the opportunity to develop reader-interaction and -navigation 
features, such as clickable index locators, embedded text 
representation, and publication/data-set cross-references, 
that go beyond commercial publishers' capabilities.

RMOS therefore hopes to offer suggestions benefitting authors 
that intend to adhere to personal extensions to formats 
such as JATS in conjunction with personal Manual of Style 
guidelines.  In concrete terms, RMOS proposes an extension 
framework called `q.Troiqa` (for `q.Tagged Research Object Infoset 
Query Abstractions`/) that merges the customization/personalization 
features of both document-oriented frameworks such as JATS, and 
data-oriented standards such as RO-Crate.  In general, `Troiqa; 
tags will mimic `JATS; tags wherever possible.  The entries in the 
current document will identify potential differences 
when recommended `Troiqa; tags/annotations differ from their JATS 
counterparts.  


`section.Preliminaries`

The following general comments apply to multiple style-guideline entries.

`quasi-section.Capitalization/Uppercase: some definitions`

In guidelines such as CMOS, the term `capitalization` generally refers to the 
practice of selecting upper-case typesetting for the first 
letter of a word; in software development the same principle is often 
called `q.ucfirst`/.  To avoid confusion, RMOS adopts the following definitions:

Allcaps:  A text segment where every letter is typeset in upper case

Allcaps-contracted:  A version of allcaps where the size of the letters are reduced relative 
the capital-letter size of the surrounding material (`q.small-caps` is a related 
practice but not necessarily identical from a typesetting point of view).  This style 
may be adopted for specialized text such as acronyms or abbreviations.  (A 
more specialized, but related concept RMOS recognizes is `q.dilation`/, where 
one capitalized letter is larger than its peers, a presentation option 
applicable for example to an all-caps acronym which is also the first word in a sentence.)

UCFirst:  A practice where the first letter in most words within a spefic 
text segment is capitalized.  The list of words that are `i.not` capitalized 
at all may vary according to guidelines, but in general includes short 
prepositions, articles, and conjunctions (to, of, in, and, etc.).

UCFirst to subtitle:  For a compound segment including a first component 
that functions as a title, followed by a second component that functions 
as a subtitle, the convention of applying UCFirst to the former 
and lower-case for the latter, apart from its first character.

Lowercase:  Follows the capitalization rules of normal writing, 
where (at least in the case of English) the first word of a sentence 
is typeset UCFirst, as are proper nouns, words belonging to 
a book title, etc., but in general letters are in lowercase form 
apart from specific exceptions.


`quasi-section.Letters, Characters, and Unicode Glyphs`

English has 26 letters, but the array of symbols one will encounter 
in a written document is typically much larger.  Nonstandard 
characters include:

Mathematical/Technical Symbols

Punctuation

Letters or graphemes from foreign alphabets/languages

Discourse artifacts such as footnote markers, soft hyphens, or (square) brackets 
surrounding/indicating emendations in a block or inline quote

In general, these may be represented in manuscripts in various ways:

XML/HTML character entities 

XML tags or LaTeX macros

Unicode glyphs

Unicode Private Use Area or Private Use Plane codepoints

Note that authors may need to consider glyphs that are visibly 
identical but have different code points.  For example, a single 
dash might represent a hard hyphen, a soft hyphen printed 
at the end of a line, or a minus sign.  Or, a dot might 
represent a period (punctuation), part of an abbreviation, 
or a decimal point.

In order to prevent ambiguities when searching text, some publishing 
software will adopt different Unicode characters for marks 
that play different roles, even if they are visibly identical.  
Authors who adopt this convention need to be particularly 
careful about content copied from other sources.

When any symbol apart from the standard 26 english letters is 
relevant to a style guideline below, RMOS will describe different 
options for representing the relevant characters in manuscript 
sources.  This will hopefully clarify what specific is being discussed.


 
`quasi-section.One Character or More?`

In some contexts in may be unclear precisely how to classify clusters of 
related symbols.  For example, the en-dash is sometimes written via two successive 
hyphen, and the em-dash via three.

Authors should be attentive to how such character-groups appear in a final 
publication, partly because they are a good example of discrepancies between 
more informal documents and technical papers.  Informal writing commonly 
adopts double-hyphens as de facto single punctuation marks playing 
the same role as m-dashes.  These double-hyphens are not n-dashes.  
In general, n-dashes are rare in professional manuscripts, only 
occurring in specialized contexts such as a numeric range 
(pages, dates, etc.).  Authors should be aware of whether they are 
using composition software that automatically converts dash-sequences 
to n- or m-dashes; if so, manuscripts should be checked to ensure 
that there is no confusion in the notation for these marks.

A related situation is ellipses, which sometimes are typeset in a compressed 
manner %-- including via special-purpose Unicode code-points %-- and 
sometimes modeled just as three (or four) individual characters.  Copy-and-pasting 
between documents with different conventions here can be a common source of errors.

A more obscure situation is ligatures, which are groups of letters often 
typeset using narrowed horizontal spacing.  The most common English 
ligatures involve the letter f, such as fi, ffi, and fl.  Although many ligatures 
have distinct Unicode codepoints, there is almost no context in which 
they are treated as individual symbols rather than separate letters, apart 
from special programming enviroments such as PDF rendering internals.  Thus, 
authors rarely need to be concerned with ligatures directly.  Unicode 
ligature glyphs almost never need to be explicitly indicated 
in manuscripts unless ligature symbols themselves are specifically 
a topic of discussion (i.e., `i.mentioned` vs. `i.used`/).  

With that said, the presence of ligatures can occasionally complicate 
interop between different software applications, so authors should 
at least be attentive when doing copy/paste.  More relevant 
for RMOS, style guidelines sometimes suggest policies for where 
ligatures should be specifically `i.not` be used.  Conversely, 
in some contexts where English has absorbed foreign loanwords, 
authors may prefer stylistic ligatures that more closely 
reflect the foreign provenance (cf., `i.fjord` or `i.diaeresis`/).
In other words, rigorous guidelines might compel 
authors to double-check their software's insertion (or non-insertion) 
of ligatures.



